\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Scope of claims.}
Our analyses are observational and all findings correlational; the cross-architecture design ($N = 2$ VLM-to-VLA transitions) strengthens external validity but remains insufficient for strong causal claims (Appendix~\ref{sec:claim_evidence_appendix}).
Training-associated effects concentrate at the readout token ($+0.069$ $R^2$ shift vs.\ $+0.015$ at image\_mean), while L1 instruction attention is inherited from base-model pretraining.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
Our $\Delta R^2$ metric provides a computationally cheap diagnostic (one forward pass plus probe fitting) applicable to any VLA checkpoint: $\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ signals VLM-to-VLA reorganization, while a flat profile suggests pre-existing action-decoding structure.
The VLA-to-VLA baseline preserving exit structure justifies staged training pipelines (e.g., OXE pretrain $\to$ task-specific finetune) over training from vision-language foundations.
Mid-layer probes outperform exit-layer probes where collapse is pronounced, motivating early-exit decoding; L1 attention fractions offer an architecture-agnostic indicator of instruction routing quality (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}).

\paragraph{Behavioral relevance and limitations.}
Trained $\Delta R^2$ correlates positively with published LIBERO success rates (Spearman $\rho = 0.583$, $p = 0.10$, $n = 9$); the trend is directionally consistent but underpowered (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}).
Evidence rests on $N = 2$ VLM-to-VLA transitions on LIBERO; we analyze only causal-decoder VLAs (flow-matching~\cite{black2024_pi0} and diffusion~\cite{ghosh2024_octo} architectures may differ), and all analyses are open-loop.
We characterize training end-states; tracing when exit collapse emerges remains for future work.

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
Across three VLA architectures and four LIBERO suites, VLM-to-VLA fine-tuning is associated with substantial exit collapse ($\Delta R^2 = 0.15$--$0.45$), whereas VLA-to-VLA conditions show only small fluctuations ($\Delta R^2 < 0.10$).
All trained VLAs exhibit medium-to-large counterfactual sensitivity ($d = 0.45$--$1.52$); L1 instruction attention is inherited from base-model priors; three NLP controls (all $\Delta R^2 = 0.000$) further support transition-type specificity.
The exit-collapse diagnostic and reference-checkpoint comparison protocol offer reusable tools for evaluating instruction processing in future VLA architectures; understanding which representational structures transfer and which must be constructed can inform more efficient training pipelines.
Code and data will be released upon acceptance.

